course: dictionary
lesson: 1885
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning the writer means. The word college is a good example. In «Lewis Carroll was the pen name of Charles Dodgson, a mathematics teacher at Christ Church college in Oxford», college means one of the parts that make up a university, not a place where teenagers study.
explain: Look for clues. In «Some say the Electoral College makes candidates care about small states», the word Electoral tells you the college is connected with voting, so it means a group of people with a special job, not a building. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence best?
explain: Meanings can also change from country to country. In the US, college usually means university, so «going to college» means studying for a degree. In the UK, college often means a school for students after age 16. If you are unsure, check where the text comes from, read on for more clues, or look the word up in a dictionary.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: College can mean a place of learning after school, a university, or an organised group of professional people.
fact: In US English, college usually means university.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, college often means a school for students after age 16.
fact: Some universities, such as Oxford and Cambridge, are made up of colleges.
exercise: Lewis Carroll was a mathematics teacher at Christ Church college in Oxford. What does college mean here? | A holiday camp for students | A group of doctors who vote for a president | A school for children under age 11 | *One of the parts that make up a university
exercise: «In the US, going to college means studying for a degree.» What does college mean here? | A group of surgeons only | A school for children under 11 | A part of a town | *University
exercise: In «Some say the Electoral College makes candidates care about small states», which word is the best clue? | *Electoral | candidates | care | small
test: In the UK, what does college often mean for students after age 16? | A university in America | A holiday camp | A type of exam | *A school for further study
test: Which country uses college to mean a university? | Australia | Ireland | New Zealand | *United States
exam: Some say the Electoral College makes candidates care about small states; what does college mean here? | A part of Oxford University | A school for students aged 16 | A place to learn to drive | *A group of people with a special job, such as voting for a president
